a.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 6 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519821 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519818 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=4$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fcd4852-bd78-56bb-b0b5-886d7bd31914" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519818 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519803 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519833 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6c/6c7fc0c4d92c68dad3d8607f25f7be80d79ca7b2d412b67b0942a669725e3d36.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=4$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e830914b-72a8-5550-9a2a-83a9bba0628e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519833 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4b3e6446a0ae02a08e91d527f402287151ddb3f39c7b744503430cb9697e03a.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(4-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 5,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(3\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519842 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186175987.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519842 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "e0a33aba-1007-58fc-bd49-8291e7b73dae" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562786 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562768 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a3db414-03cf-5eab-8959-e39595655eb0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562768 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d2/d2ee5fe463ad086cce741c62866c38c7ea331b343bdb0ae82ab64a0f8e730a1e.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-4x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+4xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 4 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(4 y^{2}-(- 4 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 4(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(4-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}4\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=16[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=16\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562773 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562784 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562773 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562773 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562773 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36d37f77-94a1-5750-955e-00c0cfbaff25" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562773 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/eed043488a6cd3949da95907125dcdfa2f946feefff60772495e48e9610c4bc1.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{1}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562790 #edb/ref 1759