 &=384 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562815 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186176023.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186562815 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "2f64dc35-1cec-5b25-aead-5876ef725a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563851 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563824 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21cd5573-a0e4-5bef-86ec-1c6e167cab52" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563824 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/47/47ee81cb3603c75c8a83dca2df1153733902576892fa6eed3df4bccd329655ee.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-18{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\n