n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563834 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563844 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563844 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨6y(x-1),\\,6x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "969a550c-c4ff-5d52-a5c3-2f0c19e8dcb1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563844 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=6x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6(x-1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((6(y-1))-(6(x-1)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(6(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563859 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186176024.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563859 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "90d0da81-d53f-5b4c-9742-9db341de53a1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563862 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563860 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ$,\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02fd691d-ae6a-5f3c-bb90-c16fde492236" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186563860 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy$.\n\nGreen's theorem states that $\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA$.\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA$.\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$.\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta$." #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
